"On the third day of the third month in the year 353, Wang Xizhi met his friends at the Orchid Pavilion in Kuaiji, near today's Shaoxing in Zhejiang. It was the day of an old spring custom: people went to the water to wash away bad luck. There were high mountains, tall bamboo, and a clear, fast stream. The friends sat along a winding stream and played a game. Cups of wine floated down from upstream, and when a cup stopped beside a guest, that guest picked it up and drank. They also wrote poems. An early record says that 26 of them wrote poems that day, and 15 could not and had to drink more wine as a penalty. Wang Xizhi described the day as “one cup, one poem.” At the end of the day, the poems were gathered into a collection, and Wang Xizhi wrote a preface for it. We call it the Lanting Xu, the Preface to the Orchid Pavilion Collection. It begins with the happy gathering and ends with deeper thoughts about how quickly life passes. The preface is written in running script. A Tang dynasty writer, He Yanzhi, counted 28 lines and 324 characters. He noticed that when a character appears more than once, each one has a different shape. The character 之 appears most often, about twenty times, and no two are alike. Count them in the gallery below: there are exactly twenty. Where is the original today? Nobody has it. Tang dynasty books say that Emperor Taizong loved Wang Xizhi's writing so much that the Lanting Xu was buried with him. What we have are copies made by hand in the Tang dynasty and later. That is why museums call the Lanting Xu “the best running script under heaven” and yet can show only copies. Later generations called Wang Xizhi the Sage of Calligraphy. In this lesson, float a cup down the stream, watch the brush write 之 four different ways, find the matching 之 in the game, and trace them yourself.": "d310508c7564",
